-- A BREACH takes a BECAUSE of its own, so in an `#ASSERT REFUSED` it can take
-- the BECAUSE written for the directive, which would then name no reason and
-- hold for a refusal with any reason: here the refusal is "wrong", and "right"
-- goes to the BREACH. So a BECAUSE in the expression and outside parentheses
-- is a parse error, at that BECAUSE. Parentheses say which is meant:
-- `(... BREACH BECAUSE "right")` gives the reason to the BREACH, and
-- `(... BREACH) BECAUSE "right"` gives it to the directive.

#ASSERT REFUSED IF TRUE THEN REFUSE "wrong" ELSE BREACH BECAUSE "right"
